{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1066720-crossover-oddities-in-not-quite-common-rules","id":1066720,"title":"Crossover Oddities in Not Quite Common Rules","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-03-22T23:43:32-07:00","id":1066720,"title":"Crossover Oddities in Not Quite Common Rules","content":"I was going over V20 and M20 when something struck me about the entry for the latter's mechanical effects of most stimulants - coke, speed, etc. - namely that while Werewolf has it that the effect is to gain a temporary Rage point and reduce the number of successes required to frenzy by one, whereas Kindred gain a short-lived additional dot of Celerity for feeding on someone who has consumed said drug, along with a +1 difficulty to resist frenzy. Normally, it'd just be a write-off, but for the sadly frequent tendency of Vampire Disciplines to treat Werewolf frenzies as if they happened in the same fashion as ones in Vampire, where you're trying to tamp it down or ride it instead of it potentially blowing up in an instant. I doubt it was the aim of the drugs and poisons entry, but it accidentally applied a standard for actually important crossover mechanics questions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}